---
import GuideLayout from '../layouts/GuideLayout.astro';

const guide = {
  title: 'Dorm Room Essentials Organized by Real Daily Need',
  description: 'A realistic, practical room setup guide focusing on sleep hygiene, study ergonomics, and functional storage rather than aesthetic clutter.',
  category: 'Essentials Guide',
  categoryUrl: '/dorm-room-essentials/',
  lastUpdated: '2026-08-12',
  readingTimeMinutes: 9,
  currentSlug: 'dorm-room-essentials',
};

const faqs = [
  {
    question: 'Why is a mattress encasement protector necessary in dorms?',
    answer: 'Dorm mattresses have been used by multiple students over many years. A full zippered waterproof encasement creates a sealed physical barrier against allergens, dust mites, and stains.',
  },
  {
    question: 'How do I optimize storage under a lofted or unlofted dorm bed?',
    answer: 'Standard dorm beds have 10 to 14 inches of clearance, while lofted or junior-lofted frames offer 28 to 36 inches. Measure clearance during move-in before purchasing rigid plastic drawers; low-profile zippered under-bed bags accommodate variable heights easily.',
  },
];
---

<GuideLayout {...guide} faqs={faqs}>
  <div class="space-y-10 text-base leading-relaxed">
    <!-- Intro block -->
    <div class="p-6 bg-cream-100 border border-cream-300 rounded-2xl">
      <h2 class="text-lg font-bold text-navy-900 mb-2">
        Why Most "Dorm Essentials" Lists Fail Students
      </h2>
      <p class="text-sm text-navy-700 leading-relaxed">
        Typical affiliate blogs publish bloated 150-item lists packed with novelty gadgets that end up in campus dumpsters by December. Our guide prioritizes items that solve four non-negotiable physical constraints: <strong>mattress hardness, communal bathroom hygiene, electrical outlet scarcity, and tight closet dimensions</strong>.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Zone 1: Sleep Sanctuary -->
    <section class="space-y-4">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold font-serif text-navy-900">
        Zone 1: Sleep Sanctuary (Where Your Academic Focus Begins)
      </h2>
      <p>
        University dorm mattresses are institutional-grade, waterproof vinyl slabs designed for durability, not spinal support. Proper bedding is the single highest-ROI investment for your health and grades.
      </p>

      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-3 gap-4 my-4">
        <div class="p-5 bg-white border border-cream-300 rounded-xl space-y-2">
          <span class="text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-wider text-forest-800">Priority 1</span>
          <h3 class="font-bold text-navy-900 text-base">2–3 Inch Mattress Topper</h3>
          <p class="text-xs text-navy-600 leading-relaxed">
            High-density memory foam or latex. A 2-inch topper provides adequate cushion; 3 inches offers hotel-like plushness for side sleepers.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="p-5 bg-white border border-cream-300 rounded-xl space-y-2">
          <span class="text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-wider text-forest-800">Priority 2</span>
          <h3 class="font-bold text-navy-900 text-base">Zippered Encasement</h3>
          <p class="text-xs text-navy-600 leading-relaxed">
            A 6-sided zippered mattress protector creates an impenetrable hygiene seal over the provided institutional mattress.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="p-5 bg-white border border-cream-300 rounded-xl space-y-2">
          <span class="text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-wider text-forest-800">Priority 3</span>
          <h3 class="font-bold text-navy-900 text-base">Two Twin XL Sheet Sets</h3>
          <p class="text-xs text-navy-600 leading-relaxed">
            Breathable 100% cotton or percale sheets prevent overheating. Having two sets ensures you have fresh bedding on laundry day.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Zone 2: Communal Bathroom & Hygiene Logistics -->
    <section class="space-y-4">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold font-serif text-navy-900">
        Zone 2: Communal Bathroom Logistics
      </h2>
      <p>
        Sharing a bathroom down the hall with 20–40 floormates requires quick, hygienic routines:
      </p>
      <ul class="space-y-2 list-disc list-inside text-sm sm:text-base text-navy-700">
        <li><strong>Quick-Drying Mesh Shower Caddy:</strong> Rigid plastic tubs trap standing water and grow mildew within weeks. Mesh caddies drain water instantly and can be hung on hooks.</li>
        <li><strong>Anti-Microbial Shower Shoes:</strong> Non-negotiable protection against communal floor infections. Look for textured rubber slides with drainage holes.</li>
        <li><strong>Waffle-Weave or Turkish Bath Towels:</strong> Thick luxury bath sheets take 12+ hours to dry in damp dorm rooms. Lightweight waffle towels dry in under 2 hours on a bedpost.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <!-- Zone 3: Power & Workspace Ergonomics -->
    <section class="space-y-4">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold font-serif text-navy-900">
        Zone 3: Power Distribution & Desk Ergonomics
      </h2>
      <p>
        Most dorm rooms have only 2–3 duplex electrical wall receptacles for two students with laptops, tablets, phones, desk lamps, and chargers.
      </p>
      <div class="p-5 bg-amber-50 border border-amber-300 rounded-xl text-xs sm:text-sm text-navy-900 space-y-2">
        <h3 class="font-bold text-amber-950 flex items-center gap-1.5">
          <span>⚡</span>
          <span>Surge Protector vs Extension Cord Rules</span>
        </h3>
        <p>
          Never buy standard 2-prong extension cords or ungrounded cubes. Residence Life inspects rooms for fire compliance: you must use <strong>UL-listed, grounded surge protectors with built-in circuit breakers</strong>. Look for heavy-duty 8-to-10-foot cables.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Zone 4: Vertical & Under-Bed Storage Optimization -->
    <section class="space-y-4">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold font-serif text-navy-900">
        Zone 4: Maximizing Limited Floor Space
      </h2>
      <p>
        When square footage is constrained, utilize vertical height:
      </p>
      <ul class="space-y-2 list-disc list-inside text-sm sm:text-base text-navy-700">
        <li><strong>Velvet Slim Hangers (30-Pack):</strong> Slim hangers increase closet hanging capacity by up to 50% compared to thick plastic tubing.</li>
        <li><strong>Under-Bed Fabric Storage Bags:</strong> Flexible zippered containers protect out-of-season clothing, extra bedding, and winter coats from floor dust.</li>
        <li><strong>Over-the-Door Multi-Hooks:</strong> Instant hanging storage for towels, backpacks, and jackets without requiring adhesive damage to walls.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <!-- Internal Linking Box -->
    <div class="p-6 bg-cream-100 border border-cream-300 rounded-2xl flex flex-col sm:flex-row items-center justify-between gap-4">
      <div>
        <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-navy-900">Track All Items in the Master Checklist</h3>
        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-navy-600 mt-0.5">Filter by budget, priority need, and print a custom checklist for your shopping run.</p>
      </div>
      <a
        href="/college-dorm-checklist/"
        class="px-5 py-2.5 rounded-lg bg-forest-800 text-cream-50 text-sm font-semibold hover:bg-forest-900 transition-colors shrink-0"
      >
        Open Dorm Checklist &rarr;
      </a>
    </div>
  </div>
</GuideLayout>
